{
  "requirement": "[`Sources`](../../rustdocs/trust_but_verify/sources/struct.Sources.html) supplies\nbuilt-in filesystem listing, reading, outlining, text search, and structural\nsearch. Construct it with the project directory:\n\n```rust,ignore\nuse trust_but_verify::sources::Sources;\n\nlet sources = Sources::new(repository_root);\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "`requirements.sdk.sources.provider` has two obligations. `Sources` must supply built-in listing, reading, outlining, text search and structural search. It must also be constructed with `Sources::new(project_dir)`. `Sources` has `new`, `list`, `read`, `parse`, `search_text` and `search_structure` in `crates/trust-but-verify/src/sources/public.rs`. I used that file only to see which operations exist, not to judge their behavior.\n\nBoth integration tests build a real `Sources` with `Sources::new(root.path())` over a temporary directory. Together they exercise each capability against the real filesystem:\n\n- **Listing:** `gitignore_is_shared_by_all_operations_with_or_without_a_git_repository` calls `list` with `SourceScope::all()` and with scoped listings. It asserts the exact included and excluded paths, and runs with and without a Git repository.\n- **Reading:** the same test calls `read`. It asserts `read` fails for ignored files and succeeds for `.gitignore`. It doesn't check the content of a successful read, but the requirement only says reading is supplied.\n- **Outlining:** `built_in_languages_and_structural_search_use_the_project_scope` calls `parse` and checks `outline().items.len()` is 3 for a plain-text file. It also checks `fingerprint_scheme()` for plain-text and Rust files.\n- **Text search:** the gitignore test calls `search_text` with a literal and a regex pattern and with scopes. It asserts match counts, `complete` and the matched path.\n- **Structural search:** the language test calls `search_structure` for Rust and Python rules. It asserts the match path, selector, excerpt, limits with `complete`, and scope exclusion.\n\nThe unit test `defaults_use_provider_primitives_and_keep_one_parse_snapshot` in `src/sources/tests.rs` also checks the provider's default `parse`, `read` and `search_text` behavior. All three tests carry `{test}` references to the subject. The tests assert real outcomes rather than just running the code, so I found no adequacy gap.",
  "references": "4d41003c77972a16520215e0fd1626c3ad65e439c7c88420a89dd4756ab62d31",
